    "created_at": "2026-03-03T11:28:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-03-03T11:28:26Z",
    "body": "> @zcorpan wrote:\n> Which tests start to pass with this change?\n```\nnavigation-api/navigate-event/navigate-history-back-bfcache.html\nnavigation-api/navigate-event/navigatesuccess-same-document.html\nnavigation-api/ordering-and-transition/back-same-document-intercept-reject.html\nnavigation-api/ordering-and-transition/back-same-document-intercept.html\n```\n\n> @noamr wrote:\n> Doesn't this change the order of things wrt `currententrychange` ?\n\nNothing that shows up in tests on our end, but yes; it changes order. I'm working under the hypothesis that `currententrychange` should behave as it does for the synchronous intercepted navigations. If we consider the four combinations we have of async/sync + intercepted/non-intercepted we see the following (and remember that [#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation) calls `currententrychange`):\n\n### Intercepted + sync\nThis if for when event's [interception state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-navigateevent-interception-state) is not \"none\" and event's [navigationType](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-navigateevent-navigationtype) is `\"push\"`, `\"replace\"`, or `\"reload\"`. In this case we'll in the current state of the spec eventually call [#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation) (either immediately or via [#url-and-history-update-steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#url-and-history-update-steps)) and after that call commit handlers. This is the behaviour I expect. Here `currententrychange` fires before commit handlers are called.\n\n### Non-intercepted + sync\nHere we call [#commit-a-navigate-event](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#commit-a-navigate-event) _before_ reaching [#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation), which means `currententrychange` fire before commit handlers, but since we're not yielding at least the promises won't have their reactions run before the `currententrychange` handlers.\n\n### Intercepted + async\nSince [#resume-applying-the-traverse-history-step](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#resume-applying-the-traverse-history-step) is async, we'll call commit handlers before `currententrychange`, but what's more important (and the bug this is fixing), the current state of the spec will call commit handlers way before [#notify-about-the-committed-to-entry](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#notify-about-the-committed-to-entry) is called, so far ahead that the promises returned from the commit handlers definitely could be resolved, which in turn means that success steps will call _before_ the navigation has committed.\n\n### Non-intercepted + async\nSame as previous.\n\nThe question then becomes, is this what we expect? That depending on if this is a `\"traverse\"` and/or an intercepted navigation we would get different ordering between `currententrychange` and commit handlers? With the change I'm suggesting we'd fix the order to `currententrychange` always before commit handlers, regardless of async/sync or intercept/non-intercept.",
